React is a JavaScript library developed by Facebook for building user interfaces, especially single-page applications (SPAs). It focuses on creating reusable UI components that efficiently update and render when data changes.

🔑 Key Features
Component-Based Architecture: UI is broken into small, reusable pieces.

Virtual DOM: Improves performance by minimizing direct DOM manipulation.

Declarative Syntax: You describe what the UI should look like, React handles updates.

Unidirectional Data Flow: Data flows from parent to child components.

JSX (JavaScript XML): Syntax extension that allows writing HTML-like code inside JavaScript.

Hooks: Functions like useState, useEffect, and useContext enable state and lifecycle management in functional components.

React Router: Handles navigation in SPAs.

State Management: Local state (via hooks) or global state (via Redux, Context API).

Ecosystem: Rich ecosystem with tools like Next.js for SSR, React Native for mobile apps.

⚡ Advantages
Fast rendering with Virtual DOM.

Reusable components reduce development time.

Strong community and ecosystem.

Easy integration with other libraries/frameworks.

🎯 20 React Interview Q&A
Basics
What is React?  
React is a JavaScript library for building user interfaces. It allows developers to create reusable components and efficiently update the DOM using a virtual DOM.

What is JSX?  
JSX is a syntax extension that looks like HTML but is written inside JavaScript. It makes code more readable and allows mixing UI with logic.

What is the Virtual DOM?  
The Virtual DOM is a lightweight copy of the real DOM. React updates the Virtual DOM first, then efficiently syncs changes with the real DOM, improving performance.

Difference between functional and class components?

Class components use lifecycle methods and this.state.

Functional components are simpler and use hooks (useState, useEffect) for state and lifecycle.

What are props in React?  
Props are inputs passed from parent to child components. They are immutable and help make components reusable.

State & Lifecycle
What is state in React?  
State is a built-in object that stores dynamic data in a component. It can change over time and trigger re-renders.

Difference between state and props?

Props: Passed from parent, immutable.

State: Managed within the component, mutable.

Explain React Hooks.  
Hooks are functions that let you use state and lifecycle features in functional components. Examples: useState, useEffect, useContext.

What is useEffect used for?  
useEffect handles side effects like data fetching, subscriptions, or DOM manipulation after render.

What is lifting state up?  
Moving state to a common parent component so multiple child components can share and synchronize data.

Advanced Concepts
What is React Router?  
A library for handling navigation in React apps. It enables routing without full page reloads.

What is Redux?  
Redux is a state management library that provides a single source of truth for application state, using actions and reducers.

Difference between Context API and Redux?

Context API: Simple, built-in, good for small apps.

Redux: More powerful, suited for large apps with complex state.

What are controlled vs uncontrolled components?

Controlled: Form inputs managed by React state.

Uncontrolled: Form inputs managed by the DOM itself.

What is reconciliation in React?  
The process React uses to compare the Virtual DOM with the previous version and update only the changed parts in the real DOM.

Performance & Optimization
How to optimize performance in React?

Use React.memo for memoization.

Lazy load components.

Avoid unnecessary re-renders.

Use keys in lists.

Optimize state management.

What are keys in React lists?  
Keys are unique identifiers for list items. They help React track changes and update efficiently.

What is code splitting in React?  
Breaking code into smaller bundles using tools like React.lazy and Suspense to improve load times.

What is server-side rendering (SSR) in React?  
Rendering React components on the server before sending HTML to the client. Improves SEO and initial load performance.

Difference between React and Angular?

React: Library, focuses on UI, uses Virtual DOM.

Angular: Full framework, includes routing, state, and more, uses real DOM with optimizations.
